# OxyOS target-runtime manifest: the Debian base the native platform runs on.
# The graphical session is not in the image yet, but the libraries it loads are.
# Do not add a display server, compositor, greeter, toolkit, browser shell or
# desktop application here: none of them run on the OxyOS display protocol, and
# scripts/check-image-config.py rejects them.
#
# One manifest for every architecture (amd64, arm64). What only one architecture has is in a
# live-build conditional, "#if ARCHITECTURES <arch>" to "#endif" (not nested); everything else
# is installed on both. scripts/check-image-config.py checks each architecture's list.

# Live system
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
user-setup

# Core system
sudo
apt-transport-https
psmisc
cryptsetup

# Boot logo (docs/adr/0017-boot-logo.mdx): Plymouth draws the theme `oxyos` straight
# through DRM/KMS, no display server. Not plymouth-themes: it brings plymouth-label (Pango,
# Cairo, GLib) and themes the image does not use. fontconfig because Debian's initramfs hook
# copies its configuration and runs fc-cache for every graphical theme, and fails without it.
plymouth
fontconfig

# System services
network-manager
pipewire-audio
upower
# Bluetooth (docs/adr/0050-bluetooth.mdx): BlueZ, which the session speaks to and whose pairing
# agent it is, and PipeWire's BlueZ plugin, which makes headphones and speakers audio devices
# (pipewire-audio depends on it; named so the audit holds that it is installed).
bluez
libspa-0.2-bluetooth
# The clock, from the network's time servers (SNTP): systemd's own client, enabled by its package.
# Settings shows whether the clock is synchronized from it (docs/adr/0019-settings.mdx); a
# machine that lost its time otherwise fails TLS, and with it the Oxy account's sign-in.
systemd-timesyncd

# What the OxyOS runtime loads: oxyos-session, oxyos-login and the Bloom host link these, or open
# them, on the machine that runs them (each binary's NEEDED and /proc/<pid>/maps, on an installed
# ThinkPad). The session and the runtime are not packaged into the image yet (docs/adr/0023-
# installing-oxyos.mdx, step 5); a machine installed from it, which they are deployed onto, must
# not need apt for its libraries.
# - Vulkan: the loader, and Mesa's drivers (Intel's, AMD's, NVK; lavapipe on the CPU), which the
#   compositor and the Bloom host's painter open for their GPU alone (docs/adr/0031-gpu-buffers.mdx,
#   decision 14). Debian's Mesa links its X11 and Wayland window-system integration, which brings
#   their client libraries: inert residue, reported by the audit and refused by its --gate
#   (docs/iso-build.mdx, "Known residue").
mesa-vulkan-drivers
libvulkan1
# - Input: libinput, the compositor's devices (it brings libwacom and its quirks).
libinput10
# - Text: ICU (Hermes and React Native's text), HarfBuzz and FreeType (the Bloom host's shaping
#   and glyphs).
libicu76
libharfbuzz0b
libfreetype6
# - Fonts the Bloom host falls back to: Noto Sans CJK (Chinese, Japanese, Korean) and Noto Color
#   Emoji (docs/adr/0041-input-methods.mdx). Bloom's own faces are in the runtime.
fonts-noto-cjk
fonts-noto-color-emoji
# - The input-method engine's run-time packages (docs/adr/0041-input-methods.mdx, decision 1;
#   tools/input-methods/build.sh's RUNTIME_PACKAGES): fcitx5 itself is built toolkit-free and
#   installed with the runtime, as Debian's fcitx5 packages link Cairo, Pango, X11 and Wayland.
#   anthy-common recommends kasumi, a GTK 3 program: archives/no-display-stack.pref.chroot pins
#   it out.
dbus-daemon
libjson-c5
libfmt10
libboost-iostreams1.83.0
libhangul1
libhangul-data
libanthy1t64
anthy-common
xkb-data
iso-codes
# - The screen reader's voice (docs/adr/0014-screen-reader.mdx, decision 3): the reader runs
#   espeak-ng for each utterance, through PipeWire's PulseAudio server.
espeak-ng

# Utilities
bash-completion
htop
curl
unzip
zip
rsync
openssh-client
wireless-tools
ntfs-3g
dosfstools

# Hardware: firmware and microcode for laptops and desktops, named here rather than chosen by
# live-build's detection (LB_FIRMWARE_CHROOT), which installs every package with a file under
# /lib/firmware: astronomy camera drivers (indi-dsi, libfishcamp1t64, libsbig4t64, which pull
# Cairo and the X11 client libraries), telephony cards (dahdi-firmware-nonfree) and the b43
# installers (they download firmware from the internet as they install). Server and data-centre
# firmware is left out too, and scripts/check-image-config.py refuses it (SERVER_FIRMWARE):
# NICs (bnx2, bnx2x, cavium, myricom, netronome, netxen), switch ASICs (marvell-prestera), HBAs
# (qlogic), BMC graphics (ast) and data-centre GPUs (nvidia-tesla-*-gsp).
#
# Most firmware packages are architecture-independent and serve both architectures: a PCIe GPU
# or Wi-Fi card works the same in an x86-64 PC and an AArch64 workstation, a USB adapter in any
# machine, and the Raspberry Pi's Wi-Fi is Broadcom's (firmware-brcm80211). What only exists in
# PCs (the CPU microcode, Intel's integrated graphics, cameras, audio DSPs, and old PCI cards) is
# amd64-only. The Qualcomm laptops' firmware (firmware-qcom-soc) and daemons (pd-mapper,
# qrtr-ns, tqftpserv) are not here yet: those machines boot only with a device tree the
# generic UEFI image does not load, and the daemons start unconditionally, failing elsewhere
# (docs/iso-build.mdx, "Architectures").
firmware-linux-free
firmware-linux
firmware-linux-nonfree
firmware-misc-nonfree
# Graphics: AMD's and NVIDIA's consumer GPUs (integrated, or on PCIe in either architecture).
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-nvidia-graphics
# Wireless and Bluetooth.
wireless-regdb
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-ath9k-htc
firmware-carl9170
firmware-mediatek
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-libertas
firmware-zd1211
atmel-firmware
bluez-firmware
# Audio amplifiers.
firmware-cirrus
# USB TV tuners.
firmware-siano

#if ARCHITECTURES amd64
# PCs only: the CPU microcode (Intel, AMD); Intel's graphics and IPU cameras, its audio DSPs
# (SOF) and its other laptop devices; Intel's PRO/Wireless mini-PCI cards; PCI capture cards.
# (firmware-misc-nonfree recommends firmware-intel-graphics and firmware-intel-misc, so Debian
# installs those two on arm64 as well: its packaging, not this manifest.)
amd64-microcode
intel-microcode
firmware-intel-graphics
firmware-intel-sound
firmware-intel-misc
firmware-sof-signed
firmware-ipw2x00
firmware-ivtv
#endif
